## Problem

A handmade-jewellery business runs on a number nobody keeps accurately: what a
piece actually cost to make. The inputs are beads, wire, chain and findings, each
bought in packs and priced per bead, per metre or per pack, each restocked at a
different price. Every design is a different combination of them, and most
designs have variants. Meanwhile the shop front — Etsy — holds its own copy of
the same catalogue, with its own stock numbers, drifting away from the workshop's
reality with every sale.

## Approach

Model the materials properly, derive everything else. A design is a list of
required materials with quantities; its cost is computed from live material
prices, not typed in. "Producing" a design draws material stock down and finished
stock up, and refuses to proceed if a material would go negative. Etsy is a
first-class but strictly bounded integration: import an existing listing into a
design, push a design out as a draft listing, reconcile stock in either
direction.

## Architecture

A Bun-workspace monorepo — `web`, `api`, and a `types` package that carries both
the domain interfaces and the form models, so a React form and the handler that
receives it cannot disagree about a field.

`api` is Hono on Bun. Domain services (`DesignService`, `MaterialService`,
`GoalService`, `TaskService`, `UserSettingsService`) sit over repository
interfaces backed by a shared `MongoRepository` base; handlers and routes are the
HTTP edge and `dependencies/` wires the container. Images live in MinIO.

The Etsy integration is a cluster of its own: `EtsyConnectionService` owns the
OAuth handshake with a dedicated `EtsyOAuthStateStore`, `EtsyClient` is the only
thing that speaks HTTP to Etsy, and `EtsyPushService`, `EtsyReconcileService` and
`EtsyStatusService` sit on top. Nothing in the design or material domain imports
any of them.

`web` is React 19 on Vite 7, with TanStack Query, React Router 7, Tailwind 4,
Radix/shadcn, dnd-kit for the draggable task board and a TipTap editor for
design descriptions.

## Decisions and trade-offs

**Cost is computed server-side in the domain, never in the UI.** The obvious
shortcut is to multiply quantities by prices in the React component that displays
them. It is also how two screens end up disagreeing about the price of the same
design. `MaterialService` recalculates a design's `totalMaterialCosts` from its
required materials whenever a material changes, including per-variant totals, so
there is exactly one implementation of the arithmetic and it is unit-tested. The
cost is that a price edit is a heavier write than it looks — it can touch every
design that uses that material.

**Restocking uses a weighted average, not the latest price.** When a material is
topped up at a new pack price, the stored unit price becomes the blend of old and
new stock rather than the price of the most recent purchase. Latest-price is
simpler and was what I wrote first; it makes the cost of a piece jump because of
a purchase that had nothing to do with it.

**Etsy is a bounded context, and its failures are contained.** Every call to Etsy
goes through the `Etsy*` services, and the OAuth handshake has its own state
store so a failed connect cannot wedge anything else. Variation import is
explicitly best-effort: a listing with no inventory record, or a token missing a
scope, returns empty variations rather than failing the design creation that
triggered it. A third-party outage should cost you the sync, not the catalogue.

**Integration tests run against a real MongoDB.** The repository layer is thin
but easy to get subtly wrong — query shape, index usage, `$set` vs. replace.
Mocking the driver tests that I called the driver. `bun test:integration` starts
a containerised Mongo via Compose and exercises it for real.

## Outcome

Runs at
[jewellery-catalogue.imapps.uk](https://jewellery-catalogue.imapps.uk) on the
same self-hosted Dokploy host as the rest of the platform. Every PR runs Biome,
a type-check, the API unit suite, the containerised-Mongo integration suite,
Playwright e2e and a `fallow` dead-code audit; merges to `main` cut a
semantic-release version, publish images to Docker Hub and deploy.

The part I would revisit is reconciliation direction. Today pushing and
reconciling are separate deliberate actions, which is safe but means the two
stock numbers can still be out of step between them.
